# Hydra PRD / new-screen UX playbook (entry rule)

**Language:** English (unless the user asks otherwise).

Use this file as an **@** entry point when the user brings a **PRD**, **feature spec**, or asks for a **new page / flow** or material changes to an existing one. It does **not** replace the rules linked below; it **orders** work so existing product UX and the **validated capabilities map** are honored before inventing scope.

## Canonical product map (mandatory first read)

- **Always read first:** **[Documentation/hydra-app-capabilities.md](../../Documentation/hydra-app-capabilities.md)** — adopted routes, shell navigation, module intent, and known gaps.
- Do **not** implement or assume flagship flows that **contradict** this map unless the user explicitly approves expanding scope **and** the map is updated in the same change (or a clearly linked follow-up PR).
- **PRD artifacts:** in-repo under **[Documentation/prd/](../../Documentation/prd/)** or an external link + stable ID provided by the user in the task.

## Always in force (do not relax)

Follow **[AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md)** and the always-applied workspace rules, especially:

- **[ux-guardrails.mdc](./ux-guardrails.mdc)** — shared/core components and tokens untouched; scope; ambiguity.
- **[hydra-ui-tokens.mdc](./hydra-ui-tokens.mdc)** — no stray design literals; typography and surfaces from tokens / figma mirrors.
- **[hydra-ux-composition.mdc](./hydra-ux-composition.mdc)** — local composition only with existing atoms and semantic tokens.
- **[figma-repo-parity.mdc](./figma-repo-parity.mdc)** — when visuals or theme must match Figma ↔ repo.

## PRD iteration — pick the path

### A) Work already exists for this PRD (branch / uncommitted / described diff)

1. Re-read **hydra-app-capabilities.md** and the **PRD** (file or pasted scope).
2. Inspect **current code changes** (`git diff` against the user’s base branch when available, or rely on an explicit file list / patch from the user).
3. **Reconcile:** Does the diff fulfill the PRD **without** violating the map or guardrails? List gaps, risks, and **better alternatives** (clearer hierarchy, safer scope, fewer nav surprises).
4. **Uncertainty:** If anything is ambiguous, under-specified, or would touch shared systems / other screens, **ask the user** before proposing final edits — do not guess.

### B) Nothing implemented yet for this PRD

1. Clarify scope (**assumptions** vs **facts**); if still ambiguous, **ask** before coding.
2. Follow **section “Discover existing UX patterns”** below, then implement per **AGENTS.md** and linked rules.
3. When the user treats work as **ready to merge / adopted**, update **hydra-app-capabilities.md** in the same PR whenever **routes, sidebar targets, or adopted behavior** change (see also `.github/pull_request_template.md`).

## 1. Clarify scope and constraints

- Restate what the PRD asks for; note **assumptions** vs **facts**.
- Cross-check against **hydra-app-capabilities.md** so new work **extends** the map coherently.
- If scope would touch **shared components**, **tokens**, **routing used elsewhere**, or **multiple unrelated screens**, **stop and ask** per **AGENTS.md** / **ux-guardrails**.

## 2. Discover existing UX patterns in the codebase (mandatory before new layout)

Before proposing new layout or new interaction models:

- **Search the repo** for similar **screens**, **routes**, **flows**, and **sections** (e.g. list + detail, settings, wizards, tables, filters, empty/error states). Prefer **semantic search** and **grep** for route keys, page titles, and feature names aligned with the PRD.
- **Reuse or adapt** an existing pattern when it is a **reasonable fit** — same building blocks from **`src/ui/antd.ts`**, same shell/layout patterns, same established states (loading, empty, error, read-only).
- If multiple patterns exist, pick the one that matches **information density**, **navigation model**, and **primary task** closest to the PRD; briefly say why.

**Principle:** existing in-repo UX is the default “design system” for product behavior and composition; do not bypass it for novelty.

## 3. No close pattern — propose the best UX under guardrails

If no acceptable similar pattern exists:

- Apply the **decision sequence** in **AGENTS.md** (especially: one **primary CTA**, full **state** coverage, no guessing on ambiguity).
- Build a **local screen-level composition** only: combine **existing** Ant Design / approved components and **semantic tokens** — see **hydra-ux-composition** and **hydra-ui-tokens**.
- Prefer **clarity, hierarchy, scanability**, and **consistent terminology** over decoration. Secondary actions must not compete with the primary CTA; destructive actions must not read as primary unless the context is explicitly destructive (**ux-guardrails**).

**You do not need new “UX theory” beyond what AGENTS + these rules already encode** — synthesize them into one concrete proposal and, if useful, 2 short alternatives with tradeoffs when the PRD is ambiguous.

## 4. Design reference branch (when the user supplies it)

If the user provides a **Figma URL**, **screenshot**, or **mock** to match:

- Read and follow **[hydra-design-to-code.mdc](./hydra-design-to-code.mdc)** together with **figma-repo-parity** and **hydra-ui-tokens**.

## 5. Optional parity pass

For design-system alignment audits (not routine PRD implementation), use **[ds-parity-auditor.mdc](./ds-parity-auditor.mdc)** when the user asks for an audit or sync check.

## Output checklist (briefly report)

After review or implementation, confirm per **AGENTS.md** output requirements:

- **Path taken:** **A** (reviewed existing diff vs PRD) vs **B** (greenfield from PRD).
- **hydra-app-capabilities.md** — confirmed still accurate, or **updated** / called out for follow-up.
- Existing **pattern reused** vs **none found** → local composition.
- **Components** and **tokens** used (existing only).
- **Shared/core components** unchanged unless explicitly approved.
- **Scope** (local vs broader impact) and any **uncertainty** deferred to the user.
